(>> Solution to Question 4:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: State and derive the Perpendicular Axis Theorem for a plane lamina and discuss its validity and limitations in engineering mechanics.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Statement of Perpendicular Axis Theorem:) Tj
T*
(   The moment of inertia of any flat planar lamina about an axis perpendicular to its plane) Tj
T*
(   \(polar axis z\) equals the algebraic sum of its moments of inertia about two mutually) Tj
T*
(   perpendicular) Tj
T*
(   coplanar axes \(x and y\) lying in the plane of the lamina and intersecting at the polar axis:) Tj
T*
(   I_zz = I_xx + I_yy) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Mathematical Proof:) Tj
T*
(   Consider a flat planar lamina of area A lying in the x-y plane \(z = 0 everywhere\).) Tj
T*
(   Let dA be a differential area element at coordinates \(x, y\).) Tj
T*
(   The distance r of element dA from the origin \(perpendicular z-axis\) is given by the Pythagorean) Tj
T*
(   theorem:) Tj
T*
(   r^2 = x^2 + y^2) Tj
T*
(   The polar moment of inertia about the z-axis is defined as:) Tj
T*
(   I_zz = Integral\(r^2 dA\)) Tj
T*
(   Substitute r^2 = x^2 + y^2:) Tj
T*
(   I_zz = Integral\( \(x^2 + y^2\) dA \) = Integral\(y^2 dA\) + Integral\(x^2 dA\)) Tj
T*
(   By standard definitions of planar second moments of area:) Tj
T*
(   I_xx = Integral\(y^2 dA\) and I_yy = Integral\(x^2 dA\)) Tj
T*
(   Therefore, we obtain directly:) Tj
T*
(   I_zz = I_xx + I_yy. \(Q.E.D.\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Limitations and Domain of Validity:) Tj
T*
(   - The theorem is strictly valid ONLY for 2D flat planar laminas where z = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - It CANNOT be applied to 3D spatial bodies \(such as spheres, cubes, or thick blocks\).) Tj
T*
(   For a 3D solid body, mass moments of inertia satisfy I_zz = Integral\(\(x^2 + y^2\) dm\),) Tj
T*
(   I_xx = Integral\(\(y^2 + z^2\) dm\), I_yy = Integral\(\(x^2 + z^2\) dm\) => I_xx + I_yy != I_zz.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 5:) Tj
T*
ET
