(>> Solution to Question 9:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Define the Product of Inertia I_xy of a plane area, derive its parallel axis transformation formula, and prove that I_xy vanishes if the area has an axis of symmetry.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition and Physical Significance:) Tj
T*
(   The product of inertia of a 2D plane area A with respect to orthogonal x and y axes is defined) Tj
T*
(   as:) Tj
T*
(   I_xy = Integral\(x * y dA\)) Tj
T*
(   Unlike area moments of inertia \(I_xx, I_yy\) which are always strictly positive, the product) Tj
T*
(   of inertia can be positive, negative, or zero depending on the quadrant distribution of area.) Tj
T*
(   It measures the asymmetry of area distribution with respect to the coordinate axes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Parallel Axis Transformation Formula:) Tj
T*
(   Let G be the centroid of area A with centroidal coordinates \(x', y'\).) Tj
T*
(   Consider parallel axes \(x, y\) with origin displaced by \(x_bar, y_bar\) such that x = x' + x_bar) Tj
T*
(   and y = y' + y_bar.) Tj
T*
(   Substitute into definition of I_xy:) Tj
T*
(   I_xy = Integral\( \(x' + x_bar\) * \(y' + y_bar\) dA \)) Tj
T*
(   I_xy = Integral\( x'*y' dA \) + y_bar * Integral\( x' dA \) + x_bar * Integral\( y' dA \) + x_bar *) Tj
T*
(   y_bar * Integral\( dA \)) Tj
T*
(   Since \(x', y'\) are centroidal axes, Integral\(x' dA\) = 0 and Integral\(y' dA\) = 0.) Tj
T*
(   Therefore, we obtain the parallel axis theorem for product of inertia:) Tj
T*
(   I_xy = I_x'y' + A * x_bar * y_bar) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Proof that I_xy = 0 for Symmetrical Sections:) Tj
T*
(   Suppose the y-axis is an axis of reflectional symmetry for the plane area A.) Tj
T*
(   This means that for every differential area element dA located at coordinate \(+x, y\),) Tj
T*
(   there exists an identical matching area element dA located at coordinate \(-x, y\).) Tj
T*
(   Evaluating the integral by pairing symmetric elements:) Tj
T*
(   d\(I_xy\) = \(+x * y dA\) + \(-x * y dA\) = 0) Tj
T*
(   Summing over the entire symmetrical domain:) Tj
T*
(   I_xy = Integral\(x * y dA\) = 0.) Tj
T*
(   Hence, the product of inertia is identically zero whenever either the x-axis or y-axis is an) Tj
T*
(   axis of symmetry.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
