(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the cross-sectional geometry, centroid, and moment of inertia for structural engineering problem 11.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Problem Definition & Structural Cross-Section Data:) Tj
T*
(   This problem investigates the geometric properties of civil engineering section 11.) Tj
T*
(   Parameters: Component dimensions, material interfaces, and reference axes established.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Centroidal Coordinate Calculations \(x_bar, y_bar\):) Tj
T*
(   Subdivide into standardized sub-areas: A1, A2, A3 with local centroids \(x1, y1\), \(x2, y2\).) Tj
T*
(   Total Area A = Sigma\(Ai\).) Tj
T*
(   First moment of area: Q_y = Sigma\(Ai * xi\), Q_x = Sigma\(Ai * yi\).) Tj
T*
(   Centroidal coordinates: x_bar = Q_y / A, y_bar = Q_x / A.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Application of Parallel Axis Theorem for Moments of Inertia:) Tj
T*
(   For each element: I_xxi = I_Gi + Ai * \(yi - y_bar\)^2; I_yyi = I_Gi + Ai * \(xi - x_bar\)^2.) Tj
T*
(   Summation yields combined section properties: I_xx = Sigma\(I_xxi\), I_yy = Sigma\(I_yyi\).) Tj
T*
(   Polar moment of inertia: J = I_xx + I_yy; Radii of gyration: k_xx = sqrt\(I_xx / A\), k_yy =) Tj
T*
(   sqrt\(I_yy / A\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Flexural Section Modulus & Design Implications:) Tj
T*
(   Section modulus Z = I / y_max governs bending stress sigma = M / Z under civil design codes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

